{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1280,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Time travel can be complicated - and complicating,\nso let's ignore all the paradoxical stuff"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6340,
    "dDurationMs": 3669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and talk about a few simple ways that you\ncan time travel without leaving your home."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10009,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11009,
    "dDurationMs": 3941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1) Do nothing - you're already traveling through\ntime! I mean, here you are - fifteen seconds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14950,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the future since the start of this video!\nEasy, huh? The point is, we're all always"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19640,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "traveling forwards through time. But that's\nboring time travel. What's interesting is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24170,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time travel relative to other people. To do\nthat..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27310,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2) Start walking, and you'll travel through\ntime relative to someone standing still! We've"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31260,
    "dDurationMs": 4319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "known for over a century that time and space\nare really just two components of a single"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35579,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"spacetime\", and the faster you move, the\nslower time will pass for you. If you take"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39449,
    "dDurationMs": 4461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a walk around the block, you'll be 3 femtoseconds\nyounger than your friend who stayed home."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43910,
    "dDurationMs": 2530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except, in order to walk around the block,\nyou had to..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46440,
    "dDurationMs": 4779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3) Stand up. You're now further from the Earth,\nand so gravity is a tiny bit weaker for you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51219,
    "dDurationMs": 3381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which means you've traveled through time relative\nto your friend who's sitting down. That's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54600,
    "dDurationMs": 4459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right, more gravity makes time slow down,\ntoo. If you stand up for a minute your feet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59059,
    "dDurationMs": 4471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will have aged 10 femtoseconds less than your\nhead. On the other hand, GPS satellites high"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63530,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in orbit experience less of earth's gravity\nand thus travel noticeably faster through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67500,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time than we do, which is why their clocks\nare calibrated to run slow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71680,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But maybe you want to time travel more than\na few femtoseconds. Get ready for your head"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75820,
    "dDurationMs": 2020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to start spinning - I mean, the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77840,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "4) Because if the whole universe were spinning\nreally fast, general relativity predicts there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82360,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be time-loops all over the place. Moving\nalong one of these loops, you'd always feel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87180,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like you were moving forwards in time, but\noverall you'd loop around and travel back"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90990,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to a time and place in your own past. It's\na little like how you can keep moving forwards"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95110,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the Earth, but Earth's curvature brings\nyou back to where you started."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98810,
    "dDurationMs": 3949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unfortunately, our universe isn't spinning.\nMaybe it would be easier to build..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102759,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "5) An infinitely long, super-dense spinning\ncylinder, which would also curve space-time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106859,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough to create a time-loop. The problem,\nof course, is how do you build something that's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111289,
    "dDurationMs": 4981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinite in size? Maybe you could just make\nit really really really big? MmmNo. If you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116270,
    "dDurationMs": 4269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tried to squeeze this time machine into finite\nspace, you'd need negative energy - something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120539,
    "dDurationMs": 4351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nobody knows how to create - to make it work.\nOtherwise you'd end up with a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124890,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wait, but what if instead of a black hole,\nwe..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127090,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "6) Built a wormhole? Wormholes are hypothetical\n(but not physically impossible) bridges through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132220,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space-time, shortcuts that can instantaneously\nconnect two different places and times in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136570,
    "dDurationMs": 3779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our universe. If you had a wormhole you might\nbe able to use it to travel into the past"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140349,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or the future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141349,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The problem is that no-one knows how to build\na wormhole. Or, once you've built it, how"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146019,
    "dDurationMs": 4191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to keep it from collapsing: as Sean Carroll\nhas eloquently written, \"keeping wormholes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150210,
    "dDurationMs": 4749,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "open requires a form of negative energies.\nNobody knows how to make negative energies,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154959,
    "dDurationMs": 3861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "although they occasionally slap the name \"exotic\nmatter\" on the concept and pretend it might"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158820,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exist.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159820,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well that's too bad... but as consolation,\nwelcome to the future! Almost three minutes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164120,
    "dDurationMs": 27360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have passed since the beginning of this video,\nand we have personal jet-packs now!"
    } ]
  } ]
}
